// Unit 2 Lesson 4 Ã¢â‚¬â€ 30-question bank


::U2L4Q01::Flexibility is best defined as {=the available range of motion at a joint~the ability to sustain aerobic activity~the force a muscle produces~movement speed}

::U2L4Q02::Mobility is best defined as {=the ability to control movement through an appropriate range~holding any stretch as far as possible~resting after exercise~maximum joint looseness}

::U2L4Q03::Which statement accurately compares flexibility and mobility? {=They overlap, but mobility emphasizes controlled usable movement.~They are always identical.~Mobility means more range is always better.~Flexibility measures muscular endurance.}

::U2L4Q04::The best first part of a general warm-up is usually {=low-intensity whole-body movement~a maximal sprint~a long painful stretch~complete rest}

::U2L4Q05::An activity-specific warm-up should {=rehearse movements and demands of the upcoming activity~avoid all movements used later~replace the entire activity~begin at maximum intensity}

::U2L4Q06::Dynamic movement during a warm-up means {=moving through a controlled range~bouncing forcefully past discomfort~holding one position without moving~performing maximal resistance}

::U2L4Q07::Static stretching means {=holding a comfortable position for a period of time~sprinting repeatedly~moving a joint as fast as possible~testing maximum strength}

::U2L4Q08::Which sequence is most appropriate? {=Warm-up, activity, cool-down, recovery~Recovery, activity, warm-up, cool-down~Activity, warm-up, recovery, cool-down~Cool-down, activity, recovery, warm-up}

::U2L4Q09::A warm-up can reasonably be said to {=support readiness without guaranteeing injury prevention~prevent every possible injury~eliminate the need for modification~make pain safe to ignore}

::U2L4Q10::The primary purpose of a cool-down is to {=gradually lower effort after activity~reach maximum heart rate~force maximum range of motion~replace sleep}

::U2L4Q11::Which is a reasonable recovery support? {=Adequate sleep~Ignoring persistent pain~Repeating maximal effort immediately~Skipping hydration in heat}

::U2L4Q12::A student feels sharp pain during a stretch. The safest response is to {=stop the movement and seek appropriate help if needed~push farther to improve flexibility~bounce repeatedly~hold the pain for one minute}

::U2L4Q13::Which warm-up best fits a planned basketball activity? {=Easy movement followed by controlled lateral steps and gradual basketball-specific actions~Ten minutes of complete rest~Only a painful held stretch~Immediate all-out sprints}

::U2L4Q14::A justified modification for limited space is to {=use controlled stationary movements that preserve the activity goal~increase speed without checking surroundings~remove the warm-up~perform risky jumps near obstacles}

::U2L4Q15::Why should range of motion vary by person and joint? {=Bodies, joint structures, experience, and current readiness differ.~Every joint has the same safe range.~All students need identical maximum range.~Range never changes with context.}

::U2L4Q16::Which observation is useful evidence during a physical application? {=RPE, comfort, control, and symptoms before and after~Only whether a friend finished first~A guessed heart rate without measurement~The brand of clothing worn}

::U2L4Q17::A student's RPE stays unusually high during cool-down. The best conclusion is {=lower intensity further, monitor symptoms, and seek help if concerning signs persist~the cool-down is definitely complete~begin maximal activity again~ignore the response}

::U2L4Q18::Which claim uses evidence correctly? {=I reduced the range because discomfort increased at the end of the movement.~Stretching is always good because everyone says so.~More range must always be safer.~I finished, so the sequence did not matter.}

::U2L4Q19::For a running session, an activity-specific warm-up could include {=gradually faster walking and controlled running drills~only upper-body static holds~lying down until the run begins~an immediate maximal mile}

::U2L4Q20::After vigorous activity, a reasonable cool-down is {=several minutes of progressively easier movement~an abrupt stop followed by another sprint~forcing a painful stretch~skipping all symptom checks}

::U2L4Q21::Which recovery choice most directly supports tissue repair and readiness? {=Time, sleep, hydration, and appropriate nutrition~Repeated all-out activity without rest~Ignoring fatigue~Using pain as the only goal}

::U2L4Q22::A student plans static stretching before an explosive activity. The strongest analysis is {=Static work may be included appropriately, but it should not automatically replace gradual general and activity-specific preparation.~Static stretching guarantees peak performance.~No warm-up is needed afterward.~The most painful hold is best.}

::U2L4Q23::Which plan shows progression within a warm-up? {=Begin easy, increase range and complexity gradually, then rehearse activity demands~Begin with the hardest movement~Change intensity randomly~Stay completely still}

::U2L4Q24::Environmental heat should lead a student to consider {=lower intensity, hydration, shade, and symptom monitoring~wearing extra heavy layers~ignoring dizziness~removing recovery time}

::U2L4Q25::If dizziness or chest pain occurs, the student should {=stop and seek immediate adult or medical help as appropriate~finish the set before telling anyone~stretch harder~increase intensity}

::U2L4Q26::The best explanation for choosing a movement is {=It matches the activity demand, can be controlled safely, and can be modified.~It looks difficult.~It forces the greatest range.~It is popular online.}

::U2L4Q27::Which comparison is correct? {=General warm-up raises overall readiness; activity-specific warm-up rehearses relevant demands.~General warm-up is maximal; specific warm-up is rest.~Both require painful stretching.~Neither involves gradual progression.}

::U2L4Q28::A complete independent plan should include {=warm-up, main activity, cool-down, recovery, modifications, and reasons~only a list of exercises~only the final intensity~no safety decisions}

::U2L4Q29::Which response demonstrates critical analysis? {=Because my balance decreased and RPE rose, I reduced range and slowed the movement to preserve control.~I chose it because it was first on the list.~I continued because stopping is always weak.~All stretches work the same.}

::U2L4Q30::Mastery of this lesson is best demonstrated by {=designing and justifying a safe sequence that fits the person, activity, and conditions~memorizing one universal routine~reaching maximum range in every joint~avoiding all modifications}



